  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The coastal flood risk will linger this week.\n\nThe risk for minor to near moderate coastal flooding will\ncontinue through most of this week, mainly impacting the \ncoastal portions of Charleston and Colleton Counties. Coastal \nflooding will be near moderate levels both Tuesday and Wednesday\nmornings with minor flooding Thursday and possibly Friday \nmornings. \n\nToday: While guidance guidance supports minor flooding with this\nmorning's high tide around 10 AM tides have been consistently \nover performing. There are a few possibilities for the over-\nperformance of the recent tides such as a slowing of the gulf\nstream or the build up of tidal waters across inland waterways\ndue to the extended period of coastal flooding. The tide\nforecast has been updated to a value of 7.4 ft MLLW, which would\nbe a 0.8 ft departure. Given the most recent high tide was still\na departure of 0.9 ft, this value is achievable. A Coastal Flood\nAdvisory has been issued for Charleston and Coastal Colleton\nCounties. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Warm and dry conditions will extend through most\nof the week.\n\nRidging aloft will build into the southeastern states, yielding\na rain-free pattern through most of the week. High temperatures\nwill climb into the mid to upper 80s daily, roughly 5 degrees \nabove normal for late Sept/early Oct, with parts of Southeast \nGeorgia reaching the lower 90s. Lows will gradually warm each \nnight, hovering in the mid to upper 60s inland and low to mid \n70s near the beaches.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Unsettled weather could return late week, and \npossibly persisting through the weekend.\n\nUpper ridge will start to weaken late week into the weekend as a \nbroad trough shifts to the Northeast. At the surface, a cold front \nwill approach the region and possibly stall nearby, before a \nstronger shortwave eventually pushes the front through early next \nweek. Deep moisture will overspread the area with ensembles \nindicating PWats exceeding 2 inches (90-95th percentile of climo). \nThis pattern will support increasing rain chances with showers and \nthunderstorms possible each day. Some uncertainties remain with the \nevolution of this system, but it currently looks like the latter \nhalf of the weekend could be the most active.",
